**Read in full:** [Total and Partial Pressure Measurement — Karl Jousten](../sources/Total-and-Partial-Pressure-Jousten.lingua#r_7SH9NGBWVVK5YN24S2VZPTW45M), [Calibration of ionization vacuum gauges — Fedchak and colleagues](../sources/Ionization-Gauge-Calibration-Fedchak.lingua#r_18KG5KWT64MPYQ4D47V1T99CKW), [Cold-atom vacuum comparison — Barker and colleagues](../sources/Cold-Atom-Vacuum-Comparison-Barker.lingua#r_63YN75QRE9J59QPJ0Y55XYR6AV) and [Glancing collisions in vacuum metrology — Eckel and colleagues](../sources/Glancing-Collisions-Eckel.lingua#r_6PJDEW50NP5XM4WPHKTQ8PRS5F).

Choose a pressure range, gas and use condition. Reconstruct one realization and one comparison, including the measurement equation, base conditions, gas-state distribution, gauge perturbation and traceability. Explain what makes the two methods independent and what inputs or assumptions they share.

Build an uncertainty account with covariance and a changed-condition test. Compare a force, thermal, ionization or atom-loss principle as appropriate to the range, without assigning every gauge the same gas response. Use actual reported apparatus conditions when analyzing source results.

Reconstruct one tractable part of the cold-atom comparison or later correction from specified inputs. If complete raw data are available only by author request, label the resulting limitation and reproduce the mathematical or published-input part without inventing observations. Compare a physical alternative model, not just a different curve fit.

**Demonstrate:** Reproducible calculations, independent limits, identified parameter/model uncertainties and a defensible statement about what the comparison establishes. Explain which observation would overturn your preferred interpretation and revise the account after criticism.

**Return:** [Industrial gases, vacuum and high-purity supplies — from molecules to useful service](../Industrial-gases-and-vacuum.lingua#r_3GHQJEYMYEN6JDKSF4PPJ403RF).
